# Testing — Swift pack

What to test and the four tiers: `testing.mdc`. This file is the Swift tooling.

- Runner: plain `swift test` (`pnpm swift:test`), on Swift Testing (`import Testing`, `@Test`, `#expect`). No filter, no `.disabled`/`.enabled` trait however spaced (`boundaries.mjs` fails one): that is the only way the fault tests run on every PR. XCTest still works beside it.
- **A run that executes nothing fails.** `swift test` exits 0 on "Executed 0 tests" (an empty or renamed test target), so `stacks/swift/run.mjs` reads the log (`count-tests.mjs`: XCTest's outermost total plus Swift Testing's, minus skipped tests) and fails on zero. It also fails when any `@Test` in the source under `Tests/` is not in the log as passed or failed (skipped is not run): a test behind `#if false` (or any `#if`) compiles away silently. The one exception is a `#if os(X)` / `#if !os(X)` block (and its `#else`) that leaves a test to the other platform's job. `run.mjs test` has a timeout (`TASKS.test.timeoutMs`). `pnpm swift:gates` proves both on a tiny package: an empty target, then a test behind `#if false`. Don't pipe `swift test` through `head`/`grep` (`testing.mdc`).
- Property tests: Swift has none in the standard library and the pack takes no dependency, so a loop over seeds with the tiny `SplitMix64` in `DomainTests.swift`; the seed is in the failure message and replays.
- Fakes of ports live beside the tests (`Tests/NoteSyncTests/Chaos/`); assert the outcome (what the fake holds), not call counts.
- **Fault injection (tier 2) seed:** `NoteSyncChaosTests.swift`, one test per `FaultKind` plus a random-script property; `boundaries.mjs` fails a `FaultKind` case no `@Test` plays (`kind: .<case>` in its body), and `run.mjs test` fails one whose test didn't run. Time is real but short (`quickPolicy`): the policy is injected, assertions are lower bounds (a backoff happened) or generous upper ones (a hung request was abandoned), and margins are wide (25x) so a loaded runner doesn't flip a result. Swift has no virtual-time bubble like Go's; a fake clock needs an "everyone is blocked" signal the language doesn't give.
- A mac-only test goes in a `#if os(macOS)` block (not `canImport`, which the check reads as "must run here") and runs in the `Swift tests (macOS)` job; keep fault tests Linux-portable (Foundation only) so they run on every PR.
